"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was significantly elevated at 160. The surgical findings included a pathological stage pT4, positive surgical margins, and lymphovascular invasion, which are adverse features. Conversely, the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and there was no lymph node metastasis documented. The biopsy showed a lower grade (Gleason 3+4) compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 3). The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nMissing postoperative PSA measurements or subsequent follow-up significantly widen the uncertainty regarding recurrence. The current horizon suggests a time frame for recurrence, but this is a heuristic range. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their trajectory would be crucial in refining this prognosis. The nodal status was documented as negative, which is a positive factor. The fixed estimated horizon is 27.99 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 22.39–33.59 months; its coverage has not been validated."
